      date: "2026-09-06",
      html: "<h2>Keep the Best Shot</h2>\n<p>The slowest part of cleanup is not deleting — it is choosing. When eight near-identical photos stare back at you, picking a keeper takes longer than removing the other seven. Keep Best turns that choice into a glance.</p>\n<h3>Size as a quality signal</h3>\n<p>Within each group, photos are sorted by file size, and file sizes are loaded before you start reviewing. For photos from the same moment — a burst, a re-save, an import — the largest file is almost always the copy that retained the most detail: highest resolution, least compression. It rises to the top as the natural keeper.</p>\n<h3>A glance, not a debate</h3>\n<p>You no longer compare photos pixel by pixel. Check the recommended shot, confirm it looks right, and mark the rest for deletion. The decision shrinks from &quot;which of these eight is best?&quot; to &quot;is this one good?&quot; — and that is a question you can answer instantly.</p>\n<h3>Works across the scans</h3>\n<p>The same size sorting applies everywhere groups appear — exact duplicates, similar photos, and screenshots all show their items with file sizes loaded, so the quality signal is always available no matter which scan you are working through.</p>\n<h3>Nothing silent</h3>\n<p>Keep Best only sorts and recommends. It never deletes on its own: every deletion still goes through your selection and a confirmation, and the removal itself runs through the system photo library.</p>\n<h3>Built for big libraries</h3>\n<p>The heavier your backlog, the more this adds up. A hundred groups that each take a minute of deliberation become a hundred quick confirmations, and an afternoon of cleanup shrinks to a coffee break.</p>\n",
