      description: "Browse your library month by month, open any photo into a swipe review, and decide one photo at a time what stays — with a filter for all media, photos or videos.",
      date: "2026-09-06",
      html: "<h2>Swipe Clean by Month</h2>\n<p>The scanners find what is repetitive — duplicates, bursts, screenshots. The Swipe view handles everything else: the ordinary months of ordinary photos, where only you can judge what still matters.</p>\n<h3>Months as sections</h3>\n<p>The view slices your library by year and month, newest first. Each month appears as its own section with its photos laid out, so a session has natural boundaries: one month, one sitting, whenever you feel like it.</p>\n<h3>Tap into the swipe review</h3>\n<p>Tap any photo to open the swipe review. There, photos pass by one at a time, and you decide with a swipe: keep or delete. Photos you leave unmarked are untouched — nothing is preselected, and nothing is deleted without your explicit confirmation through the system photo library.</p>\n<h3>Filter what you see</h3>\n<p>A media filter lets you focus the session: all items, photos only, or videos only. That turns the same monthly view into three different tools — a full sweep, a photo-only pass, or a video-only purge.</p>\n<h3>Every decision is yours</h3>\n<p>Unlike the duplicate and screenshot scans, the Swipe view makes no assumptions. It does not know a photo is &quot;bad&quot; — it just puts you in front of your own history, one photo at a time, and lets you keep or let go.</p>\n<h3>A gentle way to shrink a library</h3>\n<p>Month-sized sessions keep the work bounded. Ten minutes on the commute, one month at a time, and a library that grew for years starts shrinking on a schedule you control — with the photos you actually love still exactly where they were.</p>\n",
