      title: "Video to GIF - Pick a Segment, Set Frame Rate, Output Animation",
      description: "Convert a video clip to GIF. For chat reactions, document annotations, and social sharing - a short animation tells more than a static image.",
      date: "2026-09-15",
      html: "<h2>Video to GIF</h2>\n<p>What a static image can explain, a short animation can say better. Reaction GIFs in chats, operation annotations in documents, little highlights on social platforms - a few seconds of looping animation beats a static image in plenty of situations.</p>\n<h3>Pick a segment</h3>\n<p>Choose a start point and an end point in the video, and grab the slice you want. A few seconds is just right - too short and you can&#39;t see it, too long and the file gets large and slow to load. Reaction GIFs used in chats typically run 2 to 4 seconds.</p>\n<h3>Frame rate</h3>\n<p>Frame rate decides how smooth the animation looks. Options: 8 / 12 / 15 / 24 fps, default 12 fps. 8 fps feels choppy (smallest file); 12 fps is the balance (good enough for everyday); 15 fps is smoother; 24 fps is fluid but the file gets noticeably bigger. Reaction GIFs in chat typically use 12 fps; tech demo clips can go up to 24 fps.</p>\n<h3>Output format</h3>\n<p>The output is a standard GIF that works on any platform, any chat tool, any document editor. No player needed, no specific app dependency - drop it straight into a conversation and it animates.</p>\n<h3>Who needs it</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Writing operation instructions: clip an interaction demo instead of a stack of screenshots</li>\n<li>You can&#39;t find the right reaction in chat - clip one out of a video</li>\n<li>Sharing fun moments on social platforms, without uploading a whole video</li>\n<li>Email signature, blog embed - a small eye-catching animation</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Width</h3>\n<p>Output width options: 240 / 320 / 480 / 720 px, default 480. Bigger width means a clearer picture, but also a bigger file. 240-320 is enough for chat; 480 for documents/tutorials; 720 only when you&#39;re sharing to a platform that cares about quality.</p>\n<h3>File size</h3>\n<p>GIFs are noticeably bigger than video - a few seconds of animation easily reaches a few MB. The export screen shows the actual size; if it&#39;s too big, the three most effective fixes are: shorten the length, shrink the width, lower the frame rate.</p>\n",
