      description: "Stitch multiple videos together in order. Reorderable, batch-processed, suitable for family footage, event recordings, and recombining split long videos.",
      date: "2026-09-15",
      html: "<h2>Video merge</h2>\n<p>A handful of scattered clips on your phone - Merge turns them into one. Merge supports multi-select, the order is yours to set, and processing runs as a queue (no hard cap on the number of clips - it just depends on your device&#39;s available memory; anywhere from a few clips to several dozen is normal).</p>\n<h3>Pick and order</h3>\n<p>Select multiple videos from Photos or Files. The selected clips appear as a list - long-press and drag to reorder. The order is exactly the order of the final stitched video. To drop a clip, swipe left to delete. To start over or pick a different batch, tap reset.</p>\n<p>Note: Extract Audio has a hard cap of 20 clips (to prevent accidental bulk selections). Merge / Convert / Compress have no hard cap - only device memory limits.</p>\n<h3>How stitching works</h3>\n<p>Stitching follows &quot;head-to-tail&quot;: the end of the first clip connects to the start of the second, the end of the second to the start of the third. No fades, no shot transitions - just clip after clip, with clear boundaries. Good for chaining several clips of the same scene.</p>\n<h3>Who needs it</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Family events shot in pieces - weddings, parties, birthdays - merge then archive</li>\n<li>Lectures or explanations recorded in chunks - merge for one full watch</li>\n<li>Long videos split and uploaded separately - stitch back to the original</li>\n<li>Mixed device and resolution footage - bring everything into one file for unified management</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Merge vs. Clip</h3>\n<p>Clip trims the head and tail off one piece; Merge stitches multiple pieces into one. The two can chain: merge first, then clip the merged long piece.</p>\n",
