    appId: "image-converter",
    article: {
      section: "blog",
      slug: "compress-quality",
      lang: "en",
      title: "How to Pick a Compression Quality: The 10–90 Levels Compared",
      description: "A side-by-side look at the nine presets (10/20/30/40/50/60/70/80/90) and the 1–99 free slider, with guidance on which level fits which scenario.",
      date: "2026-09-07",
      html: "<h2>Why So Many Levels</h2>\n<p>Image compression is a lossy operation — the more detail you drop, the smaller the file and the worse the quality. The real question is &quot;how much loss is acceptable?&quot; — sharing on social media and printing photos demand very different standards. Image Converter provides nine presets from 10 to 90, plus a free slider from 1 to 99, covering everything from extreme compression to archival quality.</p>\n<h2>How the Nine Presets Actually Perform</h2>\n<h3>10–30: Extreme Compression</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>File size</strong>: 5–15% of the original.</li>\n<li><strong>Quality</strong>: Clearly visible loss to the naked eye. Best for pure-text screenshots and flat-color images.</li>\n<li><strong>Typical scenarios</strong>: WeChat stickers, long text-only screenshots, temporary sharing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, a 5 MB photo compressed at level 10 may end up only 200–400 KB. But facial features and landscape detail will blur away.</p>\n<h3>40–60: Everyday Sharing</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>File size</strong>: 15–30% of the original.</li>\n<li><strong>Quality</strong>: Visible if you look closely; unnoticeable in normal browsing.</li>\n<li><strong>Typical scenarios</strong>: WeChat Moments, Weibo, chat sharing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>60 is the sweet spot for most scenarios</strong> — quality loss is nearly invisible and file size drops dramatically.</p>\n<h3>70–80: The Universal Recommendation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>File size</strong>: 30–50% of the original.</li>\n<li><strong>Quality</strong>: The naked eye can barely tell it apart from the original.</li>\n<li><strong>Typical scenarios</strong>: Sharing where good quality needs to be preserved while keeping size in check.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Level 70 fits professional sharing. Level 80 is right for pre-archiving important photos.</p>\n<h3>90: Near-Lossless</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>File size</strong>: 60–80% of the original.</li>\n<li><strong>Quality</strong>: Almost identical to the original.</li>\n<li><strong>Typical scenarios</strong>: Archival material, pre-press preparation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Going higher than 90 rarely helps. A JPG at 100 is larger than one at 90 with no perceivable quality gain.</p>\n<h2>What the 1–99 Free Slider Is For</h2>\n<p>The nine presets cover about 90% of scenarios, but sometimes finer control is needed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>65</strong>: A touch better than 60, a touch smaller than 70.</li>\n<li><strong>75</strong>: A touch better than 70, a touch smaller than 80.</li>\n<li><strong>85</strong>: A touch better than 80 but still under control.</li>\n<li><strong>50 / 55</strong>: Find a better balance between 40 and 60.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Compare in Real Time to Pick the Right Level</h2>\n<p>As soon as you select an image, the app immediately compresses it at the current quality and shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Original size</strong> vs <strong>compressed size</strong> (KB).</li>\n<li><strong>Compression ratio</strong> (percentage).</li>\n<li>Real-time refresh — drag the slider and see the new result instantly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Don&#39;t guess the level. <strong>Use your eyes</strong> to compare and pick the right one.</p>\n<h2>Recommended Levels by Content Type</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Content type</th>\n<th>Recommended level</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Pure-text screenshots</td>\n<td>30–50</td>\n<td>Text edges need to stay sharp, but backgrounds are simple.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordinary photos</td>\n<td>60–75</td>\n<td>Balance between quality and size.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portrait close-ups</td>\n<td>75–85</td>\n<td>Skin texture cannot survive low-quality compression.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Landscape photos</td>\n<td>60–80</td>\n<td>Lots of color gradations; below 60 shows banding.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logos / design files</td>\n<td>Use PNG instead</td>\n<td>Vector art and flat colors don&#39;t belong in JPG.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Screen-recording stills</td>\n<td>40–60</td>\n<td>Heavy text and icons; low quality blurs them.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n",
    },
