    appId: "image-to-pdf",
    article: {
      section: "blog",
      slug: "image-to-pdf-guide",
      lang: "en",
      title: "The complete guide to turning phone photos into PDF: formats, ordering, and export",
      description: "How to turn your phone photos into a single PDF: supported formats, page ordering, why exporting at original size matters, and what local processing means for your privacy.",
      date: "2026-09-07",
      html: "<h2>The Complete Guide to Phone-Photo-to-PDF</h2>\n<p>Turning photos into a PDF is useful in more situations than you might think: submitting expense receipts, sending scanned contracts, organizing copies of ID documents, archiving class notes. With Image to PDF, the whole flow happens on your phone in three steps — pick photos, order them, export.</p>\n<h2>Step 1: Pick the Right Image Source</h2>\n<p>The App gives you two ways to pick images:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>From Photos</strong>: Best for photos you&#39;ve taken on your phone. Multi-select supported, and you go straight to ordering once you&#39;ve picked.</li>\n<li><strong>From Files</strong>: Best for images already saved to iCloud Drive or a local folder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For formats, the App supports JPG, PNG, HEIC/HEIF, WebP, BMP, and GIF. HEIC is worth calling out — it&#39;s iPhone&#39;s default capture format, and many online converters don&#39;t support it, or they require an extra upload step just to handle it. Here it works directly, so you skip the &quot;convert format first, then convert to PDF&quot; middle step.</p>\n<h2>Step 2: Get the Page Order Right</h2>\n<p>When multiple images become one PDF, page order is reading order. Picked images are laid out as a grid in the App, and you can long-press and drag to reorder. Before exporting, give it one pass for the actual use case:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Expense receipts</strong>: order by date so they&#39;re easy to verify</li>\n<li><strong>Contract materials</strong>: main document first, attachments after</li>\n<li><strong>ID documents</strong>: front side first, back side after</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Step 3: Export and Share</h2>\n<p>After tapping convert, each PDF page takes its size from the original image — no scaling, no margins added, so the original clarity is preserved end-to-end. The export goes through the system share sheet: save to Files, send via Mail, AirDrop to a colleague nearby — all work. The result also lands in your local history, so if you ever need to send it again, you can grab it straight from history.</p>\n<h2>Why &quot;Local Processing&quot; Matters</h2>\n<p>Most online image-to-PDF tools upload your images to a server for processing. That means your receipts, contracts, and ID photos pass through a server you don&#39;t control. Every conversion in Image to PDF runs locally on your device, and files never leave your phone — for sensitive materials, that difference matters more than conversion speed.</p>\n<p>If you handle this kind of file often, consider chaining convert → merge → encrypt: first convert to PDF, then use Merge to combine multiple documents, and finally Encrypt to add a password before sending.</p>\n",
    },
